show_check_help() {
  cat <<HELP
${BOLD}bcs check$NC - AI-powered BCS compliance checking

${BOLD}Usage:$NC $SCRIPT_NAME check [OPTIONS] SCRIPT

${BOLD}Options:$NC
  -m, --model MODEL       Model alias or canonical model ID (${BOLD}sonnet$NC default)
  -e, --effort LEVEL      Analysis depth -- output tokens + thinking budget
                          (${BOLD}medium$NC, low/min, high, xhigh, max)
  -s, --strict            Treat warnings as violations
  -S, --no-strict         Don't treat warnings as violations (${BOLD}default$NC)
      --shellcheck        Prepend shellcheck --format=json -x output to LLM context (${BOLD}default$NC)
      --no-shellcheck     Skip shellcheck static-analysis context
      --cache             Reuse cached result for identical inputs (${BOLD}default$NC)
      --no-cache          Bypass the result cache (no read, no write)
  -T, --tier TIER         Report only findings at this tier (core|recommended|style)
  -M, --min-tier TIER     Report findings at this tier or higher severity
  -j, --json              Emit findings as a single JSON object on stdout
                          (shellcheck --format=json1-compatible envelope)
  -D, --debug             Announce raw-response dump path on success;
                          dump is always written and auto-announced on failure
  -v, --verbose           Show info messages (${BOLD}default$NC)
  -q, --quiet             Suppress info messages
  -h, --help              Show this help

${BOLD}Result Cache:$NC
  Successful results are cached under \${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/bcs/, keyed
  on the script content, standard content, model, effort, and all filters --
  re-checking an unchanged script returns instantly at zero API cost. Any
  change to an input produces a new key. Use $BOLD--no-cache$NC (or BCS_CACHE=0) to
  force a fresh LLM call. Safe to delete the directory at any time.

${BOLD}Raw Response Dump:$NC
  Every API response is saved to \${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/bcs/last-response.txt
  (or /tmp/bcs-last-response.XXXXXX via mktemp if no state dir). On failure or
  --debug, the path is printed to stderr so you can inspect the raw JSON
  without re-running the check. The Claude Code CLI backend returns text
  directly (not JSON) and normally does not dump; with --json, an invalid
  response is still preserved in the dump file.

${BOLD}Model selection:$NC
  The -m value is alias-expanded then routed to the matching backend.
  Canonical names pass through unchanged. Backend routing by name prefix
  (anthropic, google, openai, ollama, claude):

    claude-*                      -> anthropic API
    gemini-*                      -> google Gemini API
    gpt-* | o[0-9]*               -> openai API
    claude-code                   -> claude code CLI (uses BCS_MODEL or sonnet)
    claude-code:<alias-or-model>  -> claude code CLI with specific alias/model
    (anything else)               -> local ollama (e.g. minimax-m2:cloud)

  Built-in aliases (extend or override in bcs.conf via MODEL_ALIASES[k]=v):

    opus       -> claude-opus-4-8
    sonnet     -> claude-sonnet-4-6        (default)
    haiku      -> claude-haiku-4-5
    flash      -> gemini-2.5-flash
    pro        -> gemini-3.1-pro-preview
    flash-lite -> gemini-3.5-flash-lite
    gpt5       -> gpt-5
    gpt5-mini  -> gpt-5-mini
    qwen       -> qwen3.5:14b
    qwen-small -> qwen3.5:9b

  Note: Ollama models named 'claude-*', 'gemini-*', 'gpt-*' or 'o[0-9]*' are
  unreachable through -m -- rename the local model if you need to target it.

${BOLD}Effort Levels:$NC
  Each level sets max output tokens and (on capable models) the thinking or
  reasoning budget. Anthropic takes one of two shapes: a thinking token budget
  on haiku-4-5, sonnet-4-5/4-6 and opus-4-5/4-6, or adaptive thinking plus the
  -e word itself on opus-4-7/4-8 and every Claude 5 -- the latter ignore the
  token budgets listed below. OpenAI reasoning_effort is auto-enabled on
  o-series + gpt-5*; Gemini thinkingConfig on the 2.5 family (except
  gemini-2.5-flash-lite) and gemini-3.5-flash*. A model in no gate is sent no
  thinking field at all. Ollama ignores both. The claude-code CLI backend
  ignores -e altogether: it always runs at the CLI's lowest native effort.

  low         Clear violations only (4k tokens, no thinking; alias: min).
              Short scripts only: on real-size scripts it raised false [ERROR]
              findings in every measured run. Do not gate a build on it.
  medium      Violations and significant warnings (8k tokens, 2k thinking)
  high        All violations and warnings (24k tokens, 6k thinking)
  xhigh       Comprehensive audit, widest coverage (40k tokens, 12k thinking)
  max         Exhaustive line-by-line audit (64k tokens, 16k thinking)

${BOLD}Tiers & Severity:$NC
  Rules carry a $BOLD**Tier:**$NC field. The check command maps tier to severity:
    core        -> [ERROR]  (non-zero exit when any [ERROR] found)
    recommended -> [WARN]
    style       -> [WARN]
    disabled    -> not reported

  Use $BOLD-T core$NC for CI gates (fail only on core violations) or
  $BOLD-M recommended$NC to skip style findings during development. Both
  filters are applied locally to the answer, from each cited rule's own
  Tier: field and any policy.conf override -- naming them in the prompt
  only saves output tokens, it is not what enforces them. A line citing no
  BCS code (the clean line), or a code with no tier of its own, is kept.

${BOLD}Policy Overrides:$NC
  Place ${BOLD}policy.conf$NC at any of these cascading locations (later wins):
    /etc/bcs/policy.conf                        (system)
    \${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/bcs/policy.conf (user)
    .bcs/policy.conf                            (project / repo-local)
  Syntax: ${BOLD}BCS#### = core|recommended|style|disabled$NC
  See ${BOLD}bcs.policy.sample$NC in the BCS source tree for examples.

${BOLD}User Rules:$NC
  Custom rules may be added in BCS9800-BCS9899 space via:
    <data-dir>/98-user.md         (single file)
    <data-dir>/98-user.d/*.md     (drop-in directory)
  <data-dir> is resolved by FHS search: script's own data/, then
  ../share/yatti/BCS/data (relative install), \$PREFIX/share/yatti/BCS/data
  (\$PREFIX defaults to /usr/local), then /usr/share/yatti/BCS/data — see
  _find_data_dir. Either path may be a symlink to user-owned files.
  User rules are included in the generated BASH-CODING-STANDARD.md and
  respected by bcs check / bcs codes.

${BOLD}Configuration:$NC
  Config files are sourced as bash in cascade order (later overrides earlier):
    /etc/bcs.conf                (system — flat file)
    /etc/bcs/bcs.conf            (system — directory)
    /usr/local/etc/bcs/bcs.conf  (local install)
    ~/.config/bcs/bcs.conf       (user — XDG standard)
  Any file may set a subset of values; unset keys inherit from earlier layers.
  CLI flags override config file settings; config overrides env vars.

${BOLD}Environment / Config Variables:$NC
  BCS_MODEL           Default model alias or canonical name (overridden by -m)
  BCS_EFFORT          Default analysis depth (overridden by -e)
  BCS_STRICT          Treat warnings as violations: 0 or 1
  BCS_TIER            Default --tier filter (core|recommended|style)
  BCS_MIN_TIER        Default --min-tier filter (core|recommended|style)
  BCS_DEBUG           Default --debug (0 or 1); announces raw-response dump path
  BCS_JSON            Default --json (0 or 1); structured JSON output on stdout
  BCS_SHELLCHECK      Prepend shellcheck --format=json -x as static-analysis context (0 or 1; default 1)
  BCS_CACHE           Result cache: 0 or 1 (default 1); see Result Cache above
  BCS_RESPONSE_DUMP   Override the raw-response dump file path
  MODEL_ALIASES       Bash assoc. array; set in bcs.conf to add/override aliases
                      (e.g. MODEL_ALIASES[mymodel]=qwen3.5:14b)
  OLLAMA_HOST         Ollama server address (default: localhost:11434)
  BCS_OLLAMA_NUM_CTX  Ollama context window in tokens (default: 40960); must hold
                      the standard (~33k tokens) plus the script plus the reply
  BCS_OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE
                      Keep the Ollama model loaded: 30m, 1h, or seconds
                      (default: 30m; -1 = forever)
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY   Anthropic API key (for anthropic backend)
  GOOGLE_API_KEY      Google API key (for google backend)
  GEMINI_API_KEY      Alternative Google key (GOOGLE_API_KEY takes priority)
  OPENAI_API_KEY      OpenAI API key (for openai backend)

${BOLD}Inline suppression:$NC
  #bcscheck disable=BCS0606  # Suppress a rule for the next line or block

${BOLD}Text Output:$NC
  One finding per line, nothing else:
    [ERROR|WARN] BCSxxxx line N: <defect>. Fix: <remedy>
  A compliant script yields the single line "$CLEAN_LINE"
  The checker is an LLM: treat the shape as a strong convention, not a grammar.

${BOLD}JSON Output:$NC
  With $BOLD-j/--json$NC, stdout contains a single JSON object shaped like:
    { "source": "bcs", "meta": { ... }, "comments": [ ... ] }
  The schema mirrors ${BOLD}shellcheck --format=json1$NC so existing tooling can
  consume findings with minimal adjustment. Info messages still go to stderr
  when verbose. Exit code 5 is returned if the LLM produced invalid JSON
  (the raw response is preserved in \$BCS_RESPONSE_DUMP for inspection).

${BOLD}Examples:$NC
  $SCRIPT_NAME check myscript.sh
  $SCRIPT_NAME check -m sonnet myscript.sh
  $SCRIPT_NAME check -m opus -e high deploy.sh
  $SCRIPT_NAME check -m gpt5 -e medium myscript.sh
  $SCRIPT_NAME check -m minimax-m2:cloud myscript.sh
  $SCRIPT_NAME check --effort high --strict deploy.sh
  $SCRIPT_NAME check -m claude-code:opus deploy.sh
  $SCRIPT_NAME check -j myscript.sh | jq '.comments[]'
HELP
}

